ime when a patient with a  certain kind of heart attack from the  time that person get to the door of the  hospital until they get a treatment that  puts a balloon in the artery to less  than 90 minutes so door-to-balloon time  less than 90 minutes to understand how  to implement some evidence-based  practices to do that the groups that  were involved in  door to balloon time really assess the  hospitals how were people already  dealing with those patients how were  they brought to the hospital how was  communication happening and what was a  regulatory environment around us  hospitals that was influencing the way  that people were conducting the patient  care and then also thinking about the  practices themselves so it's the steps  of dealing with that patient from the  time they realized that patient has a  certain kind of heart attack until the  time that patient gets the treatment how  do we look at that step be a series of  steps and get it to less than 90 minutes  so that segues into that's all assess  that's the a and that segues into I  which is innovate so that's the next  step I'm just go on and that as I  mentioned before is really focusing on  the innovation itself so there were  there was a set of steps that needed to  happen for the patient to get balloon  within 90 minutes and how can that be  tailored to the user group so people  really thought carefully about can we  have any wiggle room and add  adaptability in these steps how do we  have the nurses speak to the doctors how  do we have the emergency response  personnel speak to the people in the  hospital and there were some areas in  those steps where there could be  adaptability to make it fit to the  hospital where it was being implemented  so those a couple of examples of a and  the eye of a did great Leslie so I go to  d I guess develop develop is I think you  know an interesting step in both  examples will go to the community health  workers to sort of illustrate this idea  but as Christina described as briefly  the developed component of aided  requires us to really think very  carefully about the context context  matters the political environment the  economic environment the organizational  structural environment and so paying  attention in this stage to these  environments can help us cultivate and  in a space in which an innovation can  take root we need to be thinking about  you know who stands to lose what are the  turf battles what are the various kinds  of incentives or mow  vations in this particular environment  how do we use data not just scientific  evidence but all kinds of data to help  create a space in which an innovation  can can grab so in this community health  example there was a group of Nepal who  were interested in having the community  health workers expand the role of  practice I mentioned some of the kinds  of things community health workers do  typically and in Nepal they had been  doing distribution of vitamin A very  successfully for some period of time and  this multidisciplinary group wanted to  have the community health workers do  more in particular do case detection and  treatment of pneumonia and government  officials didn't really have faith that  these lay health workers would be able  to do case detection and treatment very  successfully a health workers typically  low literacy living in very rural areas  off and unpaid and so the the group that  was advocating for this kind of growth  and scope did a pilot study in a couple  of districts and showed that in fact  these community health workers were  perfectly capable having been trained to  do case detection and treatment of  pneumonia and so that the pilot program  really gained the credibility of the  government officials and so they made a  government decision to spread that  program to 42 districts across the  country so it's an example of sort of  taking the environment and seeing what  needed to be done and and kind of  changing it in a way to be more  welcoming of the innovation so then I'll  pick up the e the engage step and this  is really as I mentioned before really  the kind of when the rubber hits the  road I guess you say where the  innovation comes together with the user  group and so as I mentioned before this  is thinking about boundaries thinking  about how things come in thinking about  making the innovation accessible to  people who will be using it and then  ultimately getting to a place where it's  integrated into the norms and practices  of the user group so the door to balloon  example it started the innovation was  actually again evidence-based there had  been  studies of how to reduce door-to-balloon  time and then that was developed into a  set of practices and a toolkit which was  adapted to fit the hospitals but how was  it brought in and so we think about this  idea of a boundary spanner an individual  who can bring something into an  organization is welcome there somebody  already brings something in and that  could have been a quality improvement  officer sometimes it was a cardiologist  sometimes it was a person who had  familiarity with the literature but  someone who already brought something  new in so that's fit and then  introducing the innovation to the user  group across the boundary and then the  next component of engage is to translate  the material or the messaging of the  language of the innovation so that it is  accessible to the people who actually be  using it so again with door-to-balloon  it was taking that academic language and  making it something that the people who  are actually going to be implementing  those steps and really counting the  minutes found accessible and felt that  they could do and this is also a  component of engagement writ large is to  make sure that the innovation is  understandable and usable to the people  who are actually going to be using it  and then the final component of the  engaged step is to integrate so that's  actually when the innovation is not  really new anymore this is sort of  becomes part of what we do around here  that's when you transition from  door-to-balloon time of 120 minutes to  90 or less and that's the last piece of  engaged when you see that happen the  innovation has fully engaged with the  user group it has now become part of the  DNA of the organization it's a normal  part of the way that people do things  excellent thank you so much in the final  d okay and for the final d and evolve  innovations if we've done AI de well  innovations may spread across all kinds  of networks personal professional peer  networks relationships and the  devolution happens organically so maybe  if we come back to our community health  worker example one more time  in Thailand we have a good example where  the community health worker model has  been in place since 1978 and there in  that particular cultural environment the  role of community health worker is  highly prestigious and highly valued and  so for an individual to assume a role as  a community health worker they often  stay in that role for their lifetime and  when they want to retire or leave that  role often it is handed to a child or to  a family member and so we can see the  devolving of that role across  generations in that environment and that  particular cultural value and norm has  been seen as a really you know critical  facilitator to sustainability of that  model over time excellent thank you so  much well we've explored some of the  difficulties of implementation and  scale-up then we've listened to some of  your examples from around the globe and  introduce this new model and I guess I  only have one final question for you and  that is how does this model help someone  who is a practitioner or professional  charged with making change introducing  new ways of working well one of the  things that I really like about the  model is that it has these five steps  and it's we hope it's very  straightforward so it's informed by  extensive literature and talked with  practitioners and pressure testing with  scholars in the area but at the end of  the day it's five steps that we've we're  hopeful will resonate with people who  are in the field we're really saying i  have this innovation here's my user  group how can i bring these two things  together and implement this change such  that it's it's sustainable and also  scalable thank you so much yeah I might  just add that you know the request from  the Gates Foundation really was that we  create a practical model not another  sort of theoretical conceptual piece of  work that would sit out in one of those  two million articles a year that we keep  talking about but rather could be used  in the field and so that was our charge  we were very intentional and being  thoughtful about how these components  actually might be done in the field  and so in addition to the report that we  prepared for the foundation and the  peer-reviewed articles that we published  we've created a practitioners guide that  we've really worked with practitioners  in the field to develop and so that's a  resource available to people who want to  understand what does this really mean  assessment what does that look like how  might I go about doing that and so we've  got that we maybe could make available  to viewers if they're interested  fantastic Leslie Christina thank you so  much for your time and expertise today  and thank you for joining us for what we  hope is the first of many conversations  on implementing and scaling up best  practices in healthcare I'm say Hiram  mcnatt until next time
 

